::ENG2_U06_L05_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] A presentation slide about survey findings is titled "Things." Which replacement title is clearest? {
~A. School Stuff #Incorrect. This is too vague for an academic digital presentation.
=B. Survey Results Show a Need for After-School Study Space #Correct. This title identifies the source, the finding, and the topic clearly.
~C. What We Did #Incorrect. This focuses on the presenter, not the finding the audience needs.
~D. Random Results #Incorrect. This makes the evidence sound unorganized and unreliable.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which slide detail best shows credible research evidence? {
~A. Students probably want help after school. #Incorrect. This is a guess, not research evidence.
~B. Our group thinks the issue matters. #Incorrect. This is the group's opinion rather than research evidence.
~C. The problem seems big to us. #Incorrect. This is not specific or verifiable.
=D. In a survey of 120 students, 68 reported staying after school at least twice a week. #Correct. This detail includes a source type, sample size, and specific result.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which slide revision best fits an audience that needs to understand data quickly? {
=A. Show the key result in a labeled chart with a short explanatory caption. #Correct. This presents evidence clearly and efficiently.
~B. Add a paragraph with every survey response. #Incorrect. Too much text can overwhelm the audience.
~C. Use a decorative image instead of the data. #Incorrect. Decoration does not replace evidence.
~D. Put the evidence in tiny print at the bottom. #Incorrect. Evidence that cannot be read does not help the audience.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which word best completes this academic sentence: "The survey provides ______ for the proposal"? {
~A. stuff #Incorrect. "Stuff" is informal and imprecise.
~B. guesses #Incorrect. Survey data is used as evidence, not as guesses.
=C. evidence #Correct. Evidence is information used to support a claim or proposal.
~D. decoration #Incorrect. Decoration is visual style, not support for a proposal.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] A slide uses yellow text on a white background. What is the main problem? {
=A. The contrast may make the evidence difficult to read. #Correct. Readability is essential because the audience must be able to see and understand the information.
~B. The slide includes too much credible evidence. #Incorrect. The issue is visibility, not the amount of evidence.
~C. The slide is too formal for school. #Incorrect. Formality is not the problem described.
~D. The speaker should avoid using slides. #Incorrect. Slides are useful when they are designed clearly.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which choice best explains why a presenter should organize slides in a logical sequence? {
~A. A random order makes the presentation more surprising. #Incorrect. Surprise does not help if the audience cannot follow the reasoning.
~B. Slide order does not matter if the colors are attractive. #Incorrect. Visual appeal cannot replace organization.
~C. Slides should always be arranged from longest to shortest. #Incorrect. Length is not the best organizing principle.
=D. A logical order helps the audience follow the claim, evidence, and explanation. #Correct. Sequence supports comprehension and makes the presentation easier to understand.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which slide element would most improve a chart showing survey results? {
~A. A moving animation behind the chart #Incorrect. Movement can distract from the data.
=B. A clear label explaining what each number represents #Correct. Labels help the audience interpret the evidence accurately.
~C. A background pattern behind every number #Incorrect. Patterns can make numbers hard to read.
~D. A title that says "Chart" #Incorrect. This title names the format but not the meaning.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Why should the presenter name the evidence source when using survey data? {
~A. The source makes the slide longer. #Incorrect. Length is not the purpose of naming a source.
~B. The source replaces the need for explanation. #Incorrect. Evidence still needs to be explained.
=C. The source helps the audience judge the credibility of the evidence. #Correct. Naming the source shows where the information came from.
~D. The source makes every claim automatically true. #Incorrect. Sources support claims, but the presenter must still reason carefully.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which slide most clearly presents a finding? {
~A. Many students do stuff after school. #Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~B. The chart looks interesting. #Incorrect. This comments on appearance instead of presenting a finding.
~C. We asked people questions. #Incorrect. This describes a process but not the result.
=D. 68 of 120 students surveyed stay after school at least twice a week. #Correct. This statement is specific and directly communicates a finding.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which phrase is most precise for describing information collected from students? {
=A. student survey responses #Correct. This phrase clearly and formally names the type of information.
~B. kid answers #Incorrect. This phrase is informal and not appropriate for academic communication.
~C. school talk #Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~D. some opinions #Incorrect. This is less precise and does not identify the method of collection.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] A student wants to revise a slide for a school board audience. Which change is most appropriate? {
~A. Use slang to sound relaxed. #Incorrect. A school board audience requires a formal academic tone.
~B. Remove the evidence and keep only images. #Incorrect. A formal audience needs support for the proposal.
=C. Use concise wording, readable data, and formal vocabulary. #Correct. These choices match the audience and purpose.
~D. Add jokes to each bullet point. #Incorrect. Jokes can distract from the presentation's purpose.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] What is the best reason to limit each slide to the most important information? {
~A. Slides should hide the evidence. #Incorrect. Slides should clarify evidence.
=B. Focused slides help the audience process the message. #Correct. Limiting information helps viewers understand and remember the main point.
~C. Slides should be confusing so the speaker can explain more. #Incorrect. Confusing slides weaken communication.
~D. Slides should avoid all text. #Incorrect. Some text is useful when it is purposeful and readable.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which statement correctly uses evidence from a survey? {
=A. Because 68 of 120 surveyed students remain after dismissal, the proposal addresses a documented need. #Correct. This statement connects specific evidence to a reasoned claim.
~B. A study space is needed because it sounds useful. #Incorrect. This does not use evidence.
~C. The survey was done, so the proposal is perfect. #Incorrect. Evidence supports a claim, but it does not prove that every part of a proposal is perfect.
~D. Students answered questions and that is enough. #Incorrect. The presenter must explain what the answers show.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which design choice best supports evidence understanding? {
~A. Use several fonts in every bullet. #Incorrect. Too many fonts can distract the audience.
~B. Put the most important number in the smallest font. #Incorrect. Important evidence should be easy to see.
~C. Place a decorative image over the data. #Incorrect. Covering data prevents understanding.
=D. Use consistent headings, readable text, and clear labels. #Correct. These features help the audience follow the information.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which slide closing best matches a proposal presentation? {
~A. The end, I guess. #Incorrect. This is informal and does not reinforce the purpose.
~B. That's all. #Incorrect. This does not leave the audience with a clear conclusion.
=C. A supervised study space would respond to the documented after-school need. #Correct. This closing connects the proposal back to the evidence.
~D. Look at the colors again. #Incorrect. This shifts attention away from the argument.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which term means information used to support a claim? {
~A. decoration #Incorrect. Decoration affects appearance, not support for a claim.
=B. evidence #Correct. Evidence is information used to support a claim.
~C. opinion #Incorrect. An opinion may be a claim, but it is not the supporting information.
~D. transition #Incorrect. A transition connects ideas but is not the information used as support.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] A chart has no title, labels, or source. What should be revised first? {
~A. Add more background images. #Incorrect. Images do not solve the missing context.
~B. Make the chart smaller. #Incorrect. Size alone does not explain the data.
=C. Add a clear title, labels, and source information. #Correct. These elements help the audience know what the chart shows and where the data came from.
~D. Remove the chart entirely even though it contains useful evidence. #Incorrect. The better revision is to clarify the chart.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which evidence would be weakest for a research-based presentation? {
=A. A personal guess about what students probably want #Correct. A guess is weak because it is not verified evidence.
~B. A labeled survey result from 120 students #Incorrect. This is specific and research-based.
~C. A chart showing responses by category #Incorrect. A clear chart can be strong evidence.
~D. A cited school schedule showing dismissal time #Incorrect. A relevant cited document can support the context.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] How should a student adapt a slide for viewers who will read it quickly? {
~A. Use long paragraphs with several unrelated ideas. #Incorrect. Long paragraphs slow the audience down.
=B. Use short, focused text and emphasize the key evidence. #Correct. This supports quick and accurate understanding.
~C. Use unclear abbreviations to save space. #Incorrect. Abbreviations that are unclear hurt comprehension.
~D. Remove all headings. #Incorrect. Headings guide the viewer through the presentation.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which slide sequence is most effective? {
~A. Closing, unrelated image, evidence, title #Incorrect. This sequence does not build understanding.
~B. Evidence, evidence, evidence, no explanation #Incorrect. Evidence needs explanation so the audience understands its meaning.
~C. Decorative image, title, joke, closing #Incorrect. This does not present a clear academic message.
=D. Title, claim, evidence, explanation, closing #Correct. This order introduces the topic, supports it, explains it, and concludes clearly.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which revision improves academic word choice? {
=A. Replace "a lot of kids hang around" with "many students remain on campus." #Correct. The revision is more formal and precise.
~B. Replace "survey evidence" with "stuff." #Incorrect. This makes the wording less precise.
~C. Replace "proposal" with "thing." #Incorrect. This weakens academic vocabulary.
~D. Replace "documented need" with "whatever." #Incorrect. This is informal and unclear.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] What should a presenter do when a visual looks attractive but makes evidence hard to understand? {
~A. Keep it because appearance is more important than evidence. #Incorrect. Appearance should support understanding.
~B. Add more decoration around the evidence. #Incorrect. More decoration may increase the problem.
=C. Revise the visual so the evidence is readable and clearly labeled. #Correct. Effective digital design makes information easier to understand.
~D. Delete the evidence and keep the design. #Incorrect. Removing evidence weakens the presentation.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which sentence best explains the importance of sample size in a survey slide? {
~A. Sample size is only decoration for the chart. #Incorrect. Sample size gives important context.
~B. Sample size should be hidden from the audience. #Incorrect. Hiding it makes the evidence less transparent.
~C. Sample size means the number of slides in the presentation. #Incorrect. Sample size refers to participants or items studied.
=D. Sample size tells how many people provided the data. #Correct. Knowing the number of participants helps the audience understand the evidence base.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which revision best strengthens a slide for purpose and audience? {
~A. "We found stuff." #Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
=B. "Survey results show that many students need a supervised place to work after school." #Correct. This sentence is clear, formal, and connected to the proposal.
~C. "Some people said some things." #Incorrect. This does not explain the evidence or purpose.
~D. "Look at this cool chart." #Incorrect. This focuses on appearance instead of meaning.
}



::ENG2_U06_L05_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which final check best ensures a digital presentation communicates evidence clearly? {
=A. Check that each slide has a clear purpose, readable evidence, and consistent design. #Correct. These checks address clarity, evidence, and visual support.
~B. Check that each slide has as many colors as possible. #Incorrect. Many colors do not guarantee clarity.
~C. Check that the evidence is hidden until the speaker mentions it. #Incorrect. Evidence should be visible and accessible.
~D. Check that the title is vague enough to fit any topic. #Incorrect. Titles should be specific and helpful.
}
